# Copy to .env and fill in real values. Never commit the real .env file.
#
# No NODE_ENV here deliberately: the apps/web and apps/cms images set it themselves
# (production for the built images, which docker-compose.yml uses) — a shared
# NODE_ENV=development in this file was found, during scaffolding, to override that via
# env_file and break Payload's production-mode behavior (docs/ARCHITECTURE.md, Known Issue #7).
#
# Every variable is explained in docs/guide/configuration.md.

# --- Postgres (local dev only — production points at Cloud SQL instead) ---
POSTGRES_USER=polysite
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=changeme
POSTGRES_DB=polysite
DATABASE_URL=postgres://polysite:changeme@db:5432/polysite

# --- Payload CMS ---
# Signs sessions and the form tokens, and keys the encrypted settings plugins store (SMTP password, webhook secrets...).
# Never change it on a running install: stored credentials could no longer be read.
PAYLOAD_SECRET=changeme-generate-a-long-random-string
# Not read by any code today (the admin's address comes from CMS_SCHEME and CMS_DOMAIN below); kept so existing .env files stay valid.
PAYLOAD_PUBLIC_SERVER_URL=http://localhost:3001

# --- Nuxt (public site) ---
# apiBase (client/browser, via Caddy) vs apiBaseInternal (server-side SSR,
# direct to the CMS container) are deliberately different — see
# apps/web/composables/usePayloadPage.ts for why.
NUXT_PUBLIC_API_BASE=/api
NUXT_API_BASE_INTERNAL=http://cms:3001/api
NUXT_REVALIDATE_SECRET=changeme-generate-a-long-random-string

# --- Payload -> Nuxt publish webhook ---
# Called from apps/cms/src/collections/Pages.ts (and from a plugin's per-site settings when they change) after a
# publish; REVALIDATE_SECRET must equal NUXT_REVALIDATE_SECRET above.
NUXT_REVALIDATE_URL=http://web:3000/api/revalidate
REVALIDATE_SECRET=changeme-generate-a-long-random-string

# --- Redis (petition intake queue — provisioned, but nothing reads or writes it yet) ---
REDIS_URL=redis://redis:6379

# --- First-run setup ---
# When set, the first-run setup page asks for this token before it will create the first admin, so only whoever
# controls the server can finish setup. Optional on a local install. REQUIRED when CMS_SCHEME=https: without it the
# setup (and Payload's own first-register route) is refused. Generate one with:  openssl rand -base64 32
# POLYSITE_SETUP_TOKEN=changeme-generate-a-long-random-string
# POLYSITE_ALLOW_OPEN_SETUP=1 lifts the https requirement for a throwaway or staging server. Never on a real one.

# --- Backups (backup, see docs/guide/backups.md) ---
# A backup container takes a database dump plus the uploaded media every night
# and keeps them in BACKUP_HOST_DIR on the host. All of this is optional.
# BACKUP_HOST_DIR=./backups
# Major version of your Postgres server. The backup tool's client must match it.
# POSTGRES_MAJOR=16
# Cron schedule (container time, UTC). Default: daily at 03:17.
# BACKUP_SCHEDULE=17 3 * * *
# A full test restore into a scratch database. Default: Sundays 04:47. "off" disables it.
# BACKUP_VERIFY_SCHEDULE=47 4 * * 0
# Keep every backup this many days, Sunday ones this many weeks, 1st-of-month ones this many months.
# BACKUP_KEEP_DAILY=7
# BACKUP_KEEP_WEEKLY=4
# BACKUP_KEEP_MONTHLY=6
# Encrypts each archive. Store this somewhere safe: without it the backups can't be opened.
# REQUIRED for an off-site copy: with BACKUP_RCLONE_REMOTE set, a missing or weak passphrase (under 16
# characters, a placeholder, or repeated characters) makes the backup REFUSE to send anything off-site,
# fail loudly and report unhealthy. `polysite init` generates a strong one.
# BACKUP_ENCRYPTION_PASSPHRASE=
# Off-site copy, any rclone remote (Google Cloud Storage, S3, ...). Only encrypted archives are copied. See the guide.
# BACKUP_RCLONE_REMOTE=
# BACKUP_REMOTE_KEEP_DAYS=90
# A monitoring URL (healthchecks.io style): pinged on success, URL/fail on failure.
# BACKUP_PING_URL=

# --- Caddy / domains ---
# The values below are ready for a local try-out: the wildcard serves every <name>.localhost (the demo seed uses
# voidslate.localhost and second.localhost; browsers resolve *.localhost by themselves), and the admin is at
# http://polysite.localhost/admin. Production: set real domains plus SITE_SCHEME=https and CMS_SCHEME=https to enable
# Caddy's automatic Let's Encrypt provisioning (a wildcard cannot get a certificate, so name each real domain) --
# see the Caddyfile for why the scheme is explicit.
SITE_DOMAIN=*.localhost
SITE_SCHEME=http
CMS_DOMAIN=polysite.localhost
CMS_SCHEME=http

# --- Host ports (local stack only; all optional) ---
# The local stack publishes 80 and 443 (Caddy), 3000 (the site, dev loop only), 3001 (the CMS, dev loop only) and
# 5432 (Postgres, this machine only). If one is taken, move it here. To move Caddy off port 80, put the same port
# in the domains above: SITE_DOMAIN=*.localhost:8080, CMS_DOMAIN=polysite.localhost:8080, and open
# http://voidslate.localhost:8080 and http://polysite.localhost:8080/admin.
# POLYSITE_HTTP_PORT=80
# POLYSITE_HTTPS_PORT=443
# POLYSITE_WEB_PORT=3000
# POLYSITE_CMS_PORT=3001
# POLYSITE_DB_PORT=5432


# --- OpenTelemetry (optional -- unset means fully disabled, zero overhead) ---
# Monitoring is a separately-sold add-on: the actual backend (Grafana/Loki/
# Prometheus, or anything else OTLP-compatible) lives in its own,
# independently-deployed project, never this repo. Point this at that
# project's OTLP endpoint to turn telemetry on for this deployment; leave
# both empty to run with no telemetry at all (see apps/cms/instrumentation.ts
# and apps/web/server/plugins/instrumentation.ts).
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=
OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS=

# --- Email (plugins/email -- optional, unset means the plugin no-ops) ---
# Any Nodemailer-compatible SMTP endpoint: a real provider (Resend, SendGrid,
# Postmark, ...) in production, or a local catcher like Mailpit in dev. Unset
# SMTP_HOST means Payload falls back to its own built-in no-op, same as
# before this plugin existed -- see plugins/email/README.md.
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=
EMAIL_FROM_ADDRESS=no-reply@polysite.local
EMAIL_FROM_NAME=Polysite

# --- Notifications plugin (plugins/notifications) ---
# Dev only: lets Slack/Teams/webhook channels point at loopback or private
# addresses (e.g. a throwaway local receiver). Leave unset everywhere else --
# unset, the plugin refuses private, loopback and cloud-metadata addresses
# (SSRF guard) and requires https in production.
# NOTIFY_ALLOW_PRIVATE_HOSTS=1

# --- Plugins ---
# Deactivate plugins without deleting anything: comma-separated folder names under plugins/. A deactivated plugin is
# not loaded (CMS or web), but its tables, content and settings stay in the database, so turning it back on finds
# everything as it was. Never generate a migration while this is set (the CLI refuses).
# POLYSITE_PLUGINS_OFF=posts
# Local dev: false stops the CMS from syncing tables to the config on boot, so taking a plugin away can't drop its
# tables. Leave unset to keep the usual fast loop.
# POLYSITE_DB_PUSH=false

# --- Security (see internal/SECURITY_REVIEW.md) ---
# With SITE_SCHEME or CMS_SCHEME set to https the CMS refuses to start while PAYLOAD_SECRET (and REVALIDATE_SECRET,
# NUXT_REVALIDATE_SECRET, POSTGRES_PASSWORD when set) still hold a placeholder like the values above, or are shorter than 32
# characters, or CMS_DOMAIN is empty. Generate real ones with:  openssl rand -base64 48
# POLYSITE_ALLOW_WEAK_SECRETS=1 switches that check off (a throwaway staging server only).
# The largest single upload, in megabytes (default 25). Caddy's own outer limit for the API is 100 MB.
# POLYSITE_MAX_UPLOAD_MB=25
# Per-address limits on signing in (default 20 attempts per 10 minutes) and on asking for a password-reset email
# (default 5 per 15 minutes). Kept in memory, so a restart forgets them; the address is the one Caddy adds to
# X-Forwarded-For. Payload's lock on one account after repeated wrong passwords applies as well.
# POLYSITE_LOGIN_RATE_LIMIT=20
# POLYSITE_RESET_RATE_LIMIT=5
# The public site's cap on requests per address (pages only; /_nuxt, images, fonts and the health check are not counted).
# Default 600 requests per 60 seconds per address; over the cap a visitor gets a plain 429 page with Retry-After.
# POLYSITE_WEB_RATE_LIMIT=600
# POLYSITE_WEB_RATE_WINDOW_SECONDS=60
# POLYSITE_WEB_RATE_LIMIT=0 or POLYSITE_WEB_RATE_LIMIT_OFF=1 switches the cap off (a CDN or WAF in front does the limiting).
# The address counted is the one Caddy adds to X-Forwarded-For. Behind a CDN set the header that carries the visitor's real
# address, or every visitor shares the CDN's address: CF-Connecting-IP for Cloudflare.
# POLYSITE_WEB_RATE_LIMIT_IP_HEADER=CF-Connecting-IP
# POLYSITE_WEB_TRUST_PROXY=0 ignores X-Forwarded-For and counts the connection's own address: only if the web port 3000 is
# reachable from the internet without a proxy in front (the stack's compose files never do that).

# --- Settings that belong to a plugin (each plugin's README has the detail) ---
# auth:    AUTH_API_KEYS=true switches API keys on. AUTH_MAX_LOGIN_ATTEMPTS (5) and AUTH_LOCK_MINUTES (10) change the account lock.
# auth:    A Cloudflare Turnstile check on the sign-in and forgot-password pages. Set BOTH keys to switch it on from the environment (they win over
#          the keys saved in the admin); or leave them out and paste the keys under Sign-in security in the admin instead.
#          AUTH_CAPTCHA_OFF=1 is the emergency switch: no check at all, for when nobody can sign in because Cloudflare cannot be reached or the keys are wrong.
# AUTH_TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=
# AUTH_TURNSTILE_SECRET_KEY=
# AUTH_CAPTCHA_OFF=1
# search:  POLYSITE_SEARCH_RATE_LIMIT (30 searches a minute per address; 0 switches the limit off).
# webhooks:         WEBHOOKS_MAX_ATTEMPTS, WEBHOOKS_RETRY_DELAY_MS, WEBHOOKS_DISABLE_AFTER, WEBHOOKS_TIMEOUT_MS, WEBHOOKS_LOG_DAYS.
# wordpress-import: WORDPRESS_IMPORT_MAX_MB (largest export file, default 512).
# Test-only switches that let a plugin reach private addresses or plain http; never set them on a real install:
#   NOTIFY_ALLOW_PRIVATE_HOSTS, WEBHOOKS_ALLOW_PRIVATE_HOSTS, WORDPRESS_IMPORT_ALLOW_PRIVATE_HOSTS, AUTH_SSO_ALLOW_HTTP

# --- Sibling repositories (optional) ---
# If polysite-themes and polysite-plugins are cloned next to this repository instead of inside it, say where they are, and add
# docker-compose.workspace.yml to the compose command (it passes them to the image build). Unset, the core runs on its own, or uses
# a themes/ and plugins/ folder inside this one.
# POLYSITE_THEMES_DIR=../polysite-themes
# POLYSITE_PLUGINS_DIR=../polysite-plugins
